["{
  "claim": {
    "channel": "Email",
    "claim_id": "CLM-509317",
    "incident_date": "2025-09-01",
    "report_date": "2025-09-04",
    "reported_by": "Jennifer Davis",
    "IncidentDescription": {
      "estimated_damage_amount": 21040,
      "incident_type": "slip_and_fall",
      "location_type": "residential_street",
      "police_report_number": null
    },
    "InsuredObject": [
      {
        "object_id": "PROP-9350-BROADWAY",
        "object_type": "Building",
        "year": 2010
      }
    ],
    "PolicyDetails": {
      "coverage_type": "Liability",
      "effective_date": "2023-11-14",
      "expiration_date": "2025-05-13",
      "policy_number": "POL-756977389",
      "policyholder_name": "Jennifer Davis"
    }
  },
  "claim_text": "Hi, so I'm Jennifer Davis, and I'm emailing because I was told to reach out to your office about what happened at my property earlier this week—I believe I was given a case or claim number, I think it's CLM-509317? Sorry, I have all these numbers and dates floating around right now, with so much paperwork. I hope this is the right one.\n\nI own a building at 9350 Broadway here in Springfield. It's my residential property actually, built back in 2010, and I've had it insured with you since November 2023, or, well, I think my coverage started around the 14th—that's the effective date on my policy (POL-756977389). It's liability coverage, and—though I doubt this matters to you right now—I had actually just been joking with my neighbor about how complicated insurance stuff can get. Anyway, the policy should've been good at the time of the accident because, unless I'm missing something, it doesn't expire until May 13th next year. I hope that matches with your records; paperwork always makes me a little anxious.\n\nSo, what happened was, last Sunday—well, technically September 1st, if I'm reading my phone's calendar right—I got a call from one of the tenants who rents on the first floor. Apparently, someone who was visiting slipped and fell right at the entrance area. They told me the person lost their balance, or footing, I guess, and went down kind of hard. Honestly, I wasn't there when it happened, so I can only go by what they said and what I saw later. There hadn't been any spills or anything obvious from what I could tell—maybe a slight slope on the steps  outdoors, but nothing that seemed risky until now.\n\nI reported all this today, on the 4th (you'll probably see that from this email timestamp: [Email received: 2025-09-04]), but I didn't know how quickly I was supposed to contact you, so sorry if there's a delay. The whole thing happened two or three days ago, and I just didn't want to let it go unreported.\n\nI'm still getting more details about their injuries and whether they're ok (I hope so!), but already I'm hearing the repair estimates around the entrance are coming in at something like $21,000—or $21,040 to be exact, which is just... honestly, I didn't expect it to be that high for some steps and tiles, but work has gotten so expensive in Springfield. The property was valued at $463,611 last time I checked with your office, just so you know what we're talking about.\n\nIf you need anything else, let me know—I have a bunch of receipts and about four different"]

