["{
  "claim": {
    "channel": "Phone",
    "claim_id": "CLM-699674",
    "incident_date": "2025-08-28",
    "incident_type": "rear_end_collision",
    "location_type": "residential_street",
    "police_report_number": null,
    "reported_by": "David Johnson",
    "report_date": "2025-08-30"
  },
  "claim_text": "Hey, so I guess I need to talk to someone about what happened at my building earlier this week. I'm David Johnson—my policy is \"POL-314055462\". I live over on Church Street in Georgetown, house number 9600, the yellow house, you probably know it if you're ever over on this side of town, which only has residential properties.\n\nAnyway, let me just get this out, because it's all been kind of a mess and honestly I'm still kind of rattled from it. It was two days ago—I think Thursday, no wait, sorry, Wednesday, which… well if today's Friday (I mean, I told the lady on the phone all this already, and she said she'd file the report on 8/30, which is today), so the fall happened on 8/28. Sorry, I never keep dates straight, it's been a long week with the weather being all over the place.\n\nSo, what happened was, this guy, I think his name is Martin—he was visiting, and I guess he slipped on the edge of the sidewalk right by my property, but the thing is, I'm pretty sure that's technically city property. Like, the part between the sidewalk and the street; everyone always says we're responsible for keeping it mowed, but I didn't really think about liability before this. Martin went down hard—just totally lost his balance and landed awkward on his arm. I called 911 because he was in a lot of pain, and we waited for the ambulance together. The neighbors were out watching; Judy from next door actually brought out some water, so if you need to talk to anyone else about what they saw, she was there, although she's got a lot going on with her own house since she's been redoing her porch.\n\nAnyway, while I was talking to the guy from the city who came by later, he took some measurements but didn't say much about who's at fault—just that sometimes this stuff comes up and insurance gets involved. I haven't had an accident at this property since buying it, and it was built way back in 1969 (sometimes when it rains I wish we'd gotten it checked for leaks, but that's probably not important right now). The place is insured for something like $372,000 if I remember right (I always tell people it's around that when they ask about house prices in town).\n\nThe lady handling the claim gave me the reference number—CLM-699674—when I called in, so I guess you'll need that? She was super helpful, even though I kept losing my place in the paperwork. I'm mostly just worried about the liability part, because my coverage is for that? My policy went into effect back in on May twenty-seventh this year. It'll expire on the 26th two years later.\n\nRespond with the corresponding output fields, starting with the field `[[ ## claim ## ]]` (must be formatted as a valid Python InsuranceClaim), and then ending with the marker for `[[ ## completed ## ]]`."
}"]

